WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.840
Is Kiri one with Eywa? Will the tulkun abandon&nbsp;
their pacifist ways? Is there a future for Spider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.036
and Quaritch? There is still so much story to&nbsp;
tell at the end of "Avatar: The Way of Water."

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.100
Perhaps the biggest question in all of "Avatar:&nbsp;
The Way of Water" is the one posed in the opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:23.040
narration: Who is Kiri’s father? Jake Sully tells&nbsp;
us that after the death of Dr. Grace Augustine in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.380
the first film, they discovered a child growing&nbsp;
in the body of her comatose avatar. That child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.320
Kiri, is adopted by the Sullys upon her birth,&nbsp;
but her actual origin remains a huge mystery.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.040
Throughout the film, numerous&nbsp;
characters, including Kiri herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.440
speculate as to who her father might be. There&nbsp;
are so few compelling answers that even Norm is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.600
mentioned as a possible candidate. In reality,&nbsp;
no one has any idea who the father could be.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.360
Given that Kiri has strange and powerful abilities&nbsp;
and feels so close to the natural world of Pandora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.860
that she can "hear Eywa’s heartbeat," it’s&nbsp;
possible that the whole father question is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.020
a giant red herring. When Grace died, the Na’vi&nbsp;
were trying to move her soul permanently out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.420
her dying human body and into her avatar.&nbsp;
The process failed, but before she passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.400
Grace told Jake that Eywa was real. Sometime&nbsp;
afterward, the pregnancy was discovered.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.920
Could Kiri be a creation of Eywa herself,&nbsp;
the all-seeing goddess of Pandora? It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.140
very possible. While Grace’s soul was too weak&nbsp;
to survive a transfer into her Avatar, perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.540
Eywa was able to put a child in her instead. That&nbsp;
would explain the unknown father, Kiri’s powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:36.380
and why Sigourney Weaver plays both her and Grace.&nbsp;
For now, though, we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.611
"Dad, I know you think I'm crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.102
but I feel her."

00:01:45.102 --> 00:01:49.680
One of the most interesting new characters in&nbsp;
"Avatar: The Way of Water" is Spider. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.540
a human baby left behind on Pandora when&nbsp;
the RDA was sent home. And as we’re told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.280
multiple times throughout the movie, you can’t&nbsp;
put a baby in cryosleep. While there are a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.560
other humans around for Spider’s upbringing,&nbsp;
he prefers the company of the Na’vi. Like Kiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.240
he becomes a member of the Sully family. He&nbsp;
tries to fit in the best he can by adopting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.680
the dress and customs of the Omaticaya.&nbsp;
At times, he even paints himself blue.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.180
Perhaps the most interesting thing about&nbsp;
Spider, however, is that he’s the son of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.740
the villainous Colonel Quaritch. This causes&nbsp;
a good deal of drama when Spider is captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.960
by Quaritch and his cronies, but we never learn&nbsp;
about his mother. Since Spider was just a baby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.920
when the humans were sent back to Earth, he&nbsp;
would have been born on Pandora shortly before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.900
the events of the original film. That means that&nbsp;
his mother would have been part of the RDA crew.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.540
So where is she? Was she sent back to Earth with&nbsp;
the rest of the RDA? And what was the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.920
her relationship with Quaritch? For now, at&nbsp;
least, those questions remain unanswered.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.200
At the start of "Avatar: The Way of Water," the&nbsp;
Sullys are still living with the Omaticaya in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.060
the forest. Neytiri has stepped up into her late&nbsp;
father’s role as the clan’s protector, and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:02.460
mother Mo’at remains their spiritual leader. The&nbsp;
community is torn asunder, however, when Quaritch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.560
returns with a squad of avatar hitmen, all of&nbsp;
whom have been sent to wipe the Sullys out.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.640
We don’t get to spend that long with the&nbsp;
Omaticaya before Jake and his family leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.460
to join the Metkayina. And after their departure,&nbsp;
we never go back. This leaves the fate of the clan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.260
completely uncertain, though there are some clues&nbsp;
as to what might be going on back in the forest.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.700
We’re told early on that the clan’s mountain&nbsp;
hideaway is inaccessible to ordinary humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.100
Quaritch can sneak in because he’s in an avatar,&nbsp;
so with him and his squad hunting Jake, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.000
Omaticaya should be pretty safe. This&nbsp;
theory is further supported by Norm’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.080
visit later in the film. Since he’s still&nbsp;
stationed with the Omaticaya in the forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.380
you’d think he’d mention if anything&nbsp;
truly terrible were happening.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:49.080
Of course, the main RDA force is still on the&nbsp;
Omaticaya’s doorstep. General Ardmore is prepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.540
for full colonization of the planet, and the hunt&nbsp;
for unobtainium is presumably still active. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.608
all that brewing and Hometree already destroyed,&nbsp;
the Omaticaya may not be so safe for much longer.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.400
Spider gets captured by the RDA pretty early&nbsp;
on in "Avatar 2." He spends the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.240
the film in their captivity, primarily&nbsp;
as a part of his father’s hunting party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.500
While he clearly despises his captors and&nbsp;
rejoins the Sullys as soon as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.900
Spider also seems oddly helpful to the humans&nbsp;
at times. In particular, he guides them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.040
the hidden home of the mountain banshees,&nbsp;
allowing Quaritch’s squad to acquire mounts.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.660
Why would Spider help his sworn enemies&nbsp;
in this way? It’s possible that the humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.560
already knew about the banshee nest from&nbsp;
Jake’s spying in the first film. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.600
Spider’s only alternative seems to be an&nbsp;
absolutely brutal form of brain-scanning torture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.040
which looks like it might have killed him&nbsp;
if he’d been subjected to much more. He does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.640
resist helping the recoms on the reefs&nbsp;
when they start burning Na’vi villages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.900
so the best explanation is simply that the&nbsp;
poor kid doesn’t have any other choice.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.880
However, things aren’t quite so simple. When&nbsp;
Quaritch successfully claims his banshee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.700
Spider looks excited. He smiles at the sight of&nbsp;
his father soaring through the sky. Simply put,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:03.240
this makes no sense. Spider has&nbsp;
always wanted to be a Na’vi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.760
and he completely despises his father. So why&nbsp;
isn’t he enraged to see Quaritch successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.680
bond with a mount? This should be a moment of&nbsp;
outrage, of indignation that the villain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.540
the story gets to do something spectacular&nbsp;
that Spider can never have. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.140
we don’t get enough reflection on this&nbsp;
moment to fully understand what’s going on.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.180
It’s no secret that Kiri is different than&nbsp;
the other Na’vi on Pandora. She has an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.320
uncanny ability to influence whatever&nbsp;
natural forces are around her. Fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.600
grass, trees, birds, all these and&nbsp;
more seem to bend to her will. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.440
as if the very energy of Pandora flows&nbsp;
through her into everything around her.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.520
This makes it even more curious when,&nbsp;
after bonding with the Metkayina’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.420
underwater spirit tree, Kiri has a&nbsp;
massive seizure. During the bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.860
she has a vision of her mother and asks&nbsp;
her questions about her father. But just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.720
as Grace is about to answer, she’s yanked&nbsp;
away, and Kiri’s body starts to convulse.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.040
Norm claims that the seizure is the result&nbsp;
of epilepsy and that the tree triggered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.660
hallucinations via electrical impulses&nbsp;
in Kiri’s brain. But this is Pandora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.940
and nothing is ever that simple. The spirit&nbsp;
tree is supposed to be a strong link to Eywa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.740
so maybe it’s that connection that causes the&nbsp;
seizure. Kiri seems to be a conduit for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.912
power of the planet, so linking with it in&nbsp;
such a strong way may have overloaded her.

00:06:21.432 --> 00:06:23.760
Throughout the original "Avatar" and its sequel,

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.240
we only get hints at what life is like&nbsp;
on Earth. From what is revealed, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.800
it doesn’t sound good. Jake says in the first film&nbsp;
that there’s "no green" left on his homeworld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:37.260
and General Ardmore says that the Earth is dying.&nbsp;
The plans for a colonized Pandora show how extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:42.060
things must be on Earth, with humanity soon&nbsp;
intending to leave the planet for good. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.820
we still haven’t gotten a real look&nbsp;
at what things are like back home.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:50.400
Supplemental sources like the mobile game "Avatar:&nbsp;
Pandora Rising" and the official companion book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.440
"Avatar: An Activist Survival Guide" offer&nbsp;
a few more details. Corporate dominance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.720
environmental devastation, and constant warfare&nbsp;
have effectively decimated Earth’s natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.060
ecosystem by the time the films take place. We&nbsp;
also know from a handful of lines that classism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:11.340
is rampant. The super-rich can afford miraculous&nbsp;
surgeries and eternal youth with enough money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.420
while the masses are left to fight for&nbsp;
survival on the planet’s desolate husk.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:19.560
Going back to Earth seems counterintuitive&nbsp;
to what the "Avatar" movies excel at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.940
which is showing gorgeous alien&nbsp;
locales and exotic animals. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.253
a trip home and a closer look at Earth&nbsp;
seem inevitable as the story moves forward.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.360
When Quaritch returns to Pandora at the beginning&nbsp;
of "Avatar: The Way of Water," he’s quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.220
brought up to speed by General Ardmore. She&nbsp;
informs him that the RDA’s main objective now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:42.780
is no longer the mining of unobtainium,&nbsp;
but the preparation of Pandora for full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.240
human colonization. With the Earth rapidly dying,&nbsp;
Pandora looks to be the best chance humanity has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.740
for a new home. Of course, that also means ripping&nbsp;
the planet away from its rightful inhabitants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:58.398
decimating its natural landscape, and being&nbsp;
completely vile in basically every way.

00:07:58.398 --> 00:08:01.861
"The humans are returning. They're hunting us."

00:08:01.861 --> 00:08:04.380
The odd thing is that, later&nbsp;
in the film, we see another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:09.720
very different part of the RDA’s operations,&nbsp;
the hunting of the Tulkun. The hyper-intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.140
whale-like creatures are valuable for&nbsp;
the fluid extracted from their brains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:18.780
which is said to "stop human aging" completely.&nbsp;
Like unobtainium, it’s a valuable resource,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.200
but not one that would likely benefit&nbsp;
the majority of humans on Earth.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:27.420
The RDA is pretty clearly two-faced, claiming&nbsp;
to be fighting for the survival of the species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.860
while using its immense resources to&nbsp;
further enrich Earth’s elite. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.120
duplicitous nature begs the question, who&nbsp;
would actually get to come to Pandora if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.560
it were colonized? The masses? Or just the&nbsp;
rich, propped up by their hoarded wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.880
and a healthy number of Na’vi slaves? Ardmore&nbsp;
only has a small role in "The Way of Water,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.048
but she’ll likely be much more important&nbsp;
and villainous in the following films.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.480
One of the main storylines in "Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water" is the adorable friendship between Lo’ak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:00.780
Jake and Neytiri’s second son, and Payakan, an&nbsp;
outcast Tulkun who saves his life. The tulkun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.520
has been ostracized by the rest of his kind&nbsp;
for rejecting their strict policy of pacifism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:10.680
Specifically, he led an attack on an RDA&nbsp;
ship after his mother was killed by humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.700
leading to the deaths of many&nbsp;
other Tulkun and Metkayina Na’vi.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.260
It’s a tragic story, but the timeline of it&nbsp;
is pretty confusing. Humans only returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.280
to Pandora a year before the events&nbsp;
of the film, and as far as we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:28.080
the RDA’s operations during the first film&nbsp;
had nothing to do with the sea. That means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.380
that it would have only been a year ago&nbsp;
at most that Payakan’s mother was killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.700
but that doesn’t really make sense. We see&nbsp;
that the Tulkun hunters target mothers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.860
young calves because they won’t abandon their&nbsp;
kids. While never explicitly confirmed, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.180
implied that that’s what happened to Payakan’s&nbsp;
mother. And yet, he’s a full-grown adult.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.420
Maybe he was already fully grown&nbsp;
when his mother was killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.700
but that also creates some weird discrepancies.&nbsp;
The Metkayina talk about him like he’s a legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.740
someone they grew up hearing stories about.&nbsp;
So was Tulkun hunting a thing during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.268
original "Avatar?" Is there something else&nbsp;
going on here? Or is this just a plot hole?

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:08.220
After a long battle with Jake Sully at the&nbsp;
end of "Avatar: The Way of Water," Quaritch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:12.420
is strangled out and left to drown. Of course,&nbsp;
the unkillable man makes it out once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:18.240
this time saved reluctantly by his son. Spider&nbsp;
doesn’t seek his father out. He’s looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.520
the other Sullys when he comes upon Quaritch’s&nbsp;
comatose body. Despite his hatred for the man, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:28.620
grabs him and carries him to the surface, allowing&nbsp;
him to fly to safety on the back of his banshee.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.600
Why does Spider do this? Well, there are a&nbsp;
couple of explanations. Just moments prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:38.760
he was under Neytiri’s knife, who threatened to&nbsp;
kill him if Quaritch didn’t release Tuk. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:43.500
surprising twist of fate, the villain agrees,&nbsp;
showing that he does care for his son despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:47.940
his claims to the contrary. In addition to&nbsp;
this recent display of affection, Spider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.200
probably just doesn’t want to be responsible&nbsp;
for his father’s death. Leaving him to drown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.020
would basically be killing him, and killing your&nbsp;
father is a big ask, even if you loathe his guts.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:01.140
Spider hisses at Quaritch when&nbsp;
he’s invited to flee with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.080
choosing instead to rejoin the Sullys. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.220
he doesn’t tell them what he’s done. Will&nbsp;
Quaritch continue to torment the family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:13.320
putting blood on Spider’s hands? Or will this&nbsp;
experience cause him to rethink his heinous ways?

00:11:13.808 --> 00:11:19.200
Quaritch is about as cartoonish as a movie villain&nbsp;
can possibly get. Stephen Lang is masterful as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:25.020
detestable baddie, spewing hateful monologues and&nbsp;
cheesy one-liners in the same breath. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:29.400
despite how over-the-top hateable the character&nbsp;
is, there are hints that he could redeem himself.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:34.140
In his first real conversation with Spider,&nbsp;
Quaritch apologizes for being a bad father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.320
something that doesn’t make sense&nbsp;
given that he was killed when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:41.040
son was just a baby. This whole scene&nbsp;
is a pretty obvious manipulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.127
and he even drops the same line he&nbsp;
used on Jake in the original film.

00:11:45.127 --> 00:11:47.700
"You've got some heart, kid,&nbsp;
showing up in this neighborhood."

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:52.200
Spider doesn’t buy it, but he chooses to&nbsp;
go with Quaritch rather than be subjected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.680
to more of the RDA’s torture.&nbsp;
However, by the end of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.860
there’s at least a shred of begrudging&nbsp;
loyalty between the two of them.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.600
Calling it love might be a step too far, but&nbsp;
there’s something there. Maybe Spider’s hatred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.680
of him will be the fuel that makes Quaritch&nbsp;
reflect on his past actions. Given all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.820
evil he’s done, though, full redemption&nbsp;
would be a tall task. At the end of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.600
Way of Water," Quaritch says that he and&nbsp;
Spider "aren’t even the same species,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.040
and that line has more meaning than it may&nbsp;
seem. Though he’s the one in a Na’vi body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:24.840
he clearly thinks of himself as human and&nbsp;
his son as something else. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:29.268
he surprises himself and everyone else&nbsp;
by actually begging for Spider’s life.

00:12:29.268 --> 00:12:34.860
When Payakan leaps out of the water to attack the&nbsp;
RDA ship at the end of "Avatar 2," you might think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:39.300
that the rest of the Tulkun are close behind&nbsp;
them. Sure, they despise violence and killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:44.340
but they’ll make an exception to defend&nbsp;
their home, right? Not exactly. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:48.600
one Tulkun proves to be enough, because&nbsp;
Payakan’s brethren never surface during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.260
the final battle. Whether or not they’ll&nbsp;
change their ways remains to be seen.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.040
To be clear, there can absolutely be&nbsp;
a role for nonviolent resistors in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.120
instances of oppression. But when the very&nbsp;
survival of your species is on the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:04.800
that role can be hard to find. Humanity&nbsp;
isn’t going to stop their conquest with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.480
few whales and a little bit of forest. They’re&nbsp;
coming for the whole planet. And when they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.740
a whole herd of Tulkun could be a&nbsp;
valuable weapon in the fight for freedom.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:18.480
Maybe Payakan can convince his community&nbsp;
to break their rules just this once. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.480
the very least, it would be nice to&nbsp;
see him readmitted into their ranks.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:26.040
The first "Avatar" doesn’t provide a lot of&nbsp;
details about how the Tree of Souls works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:29.400
but "The Way of Water" gives us some&nbsp;
interesting new clues through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:34.140
Metkayina’s underwater spirit tree. We see&nbsp;
multiple characters bond with the tree and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:38.160
have curious visions. However, it’s still&nbsp;
not entirely clear what they’re seeing.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.960
When Kiri bonds with the tree, she sees&nbsp;
her mother working in a lab. The two have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.760
a conversation that’s quickly cut off after&nbsp;
Kiri starts asking questions. Grace appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:52.020
to be yanked away from her, and Kiri starts&nbsp;
convulsing from a seizure in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:56.880
Later, at the end of the movie, Jake and&nbsp;
Neytiri bond with the tree and see their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:01.320
son Neteyam. This vision is different from&nbsp;
Kiri’s because it appears to be a memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:06.120
an echo of a moment at the start of the film&nbsp;
that shows Jake training Neteyam how to fish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:10.080
Because Kiri never met her mother, her&nbsp;
vision couldn’t possibly be a memory.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:14.280
So what’s happening here? Are Jake&nbsp;
and Neytiri actually communing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:18.480
their son’s spirit? Is Grace’s soul&nbsp;
still alive in Eywa as a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:22.560
how she died? The spiritual aspects of&nbsp;
Pandora may never be fully explained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:27.031
which isn’t a bad thing. But in this case,&nbsp;
it would be great to learn a little bit more.

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:31.500
The Na’vi rid their planet of humans at the&nbsp;
end of the original "Avatar," but things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:36.600
aren’t so simple in the sequel. The big action&nbsp;
climax sees the RDA’s whaling ship destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:42.120
but humanity’s operations back in the forest&nbsp;
are presumably still going strong. So what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.780
next for the RDA, and how will they&nbsp;
respond to the battle on the reefs?

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:51.240
If General Ardmore is to be believed, more human&nbsp;
forces are coming. She says that she’s been sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:57.060
to "tame this frontier," gesturing to Pandora&nbsp;
at large. It’s to be the new home for humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:02.820
if the RDA get their way, which would surely doom&nbsp;
the planet. With the very survival of the species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.200
apparently at stake, we’ve likely only seen a&nbsp;
fraction of the military might that’s coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.540
The Na’vi will have a big fight on their hands if&nbsp;
the colonization efforts continue to gain steam.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:17.580
How that fight takes shape, however, remains to&nbsp;
be seen. Could humanity create a whole army of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:23.340
avatar soldiers to adapt to Pandora’s environment?&nbsp;
It seems likely. Could they start plundering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:29.400
spiritual energy of the planet in addition to its&nbsp;
other natural resources? Also a possibility. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:33.922
future looks grim for the Na’vi, but with&nbsp;
a united front, they still have a chance.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.280
Though he’s a trained soldier, Jake Sully&nbsp;
is running scared for most of "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:42.120
The Way of Water." When he realizes how&nbsp;
great the danger to his children is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:48.120
he completely abandons his crusade against the&nbsp;
RDA and dedicates all his energy to keeping his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:52.860
family safe. Throughout the film, we see Jake&nbsp;
humble himself to the Metkayina, Quaritch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:57.600
and others to protect his own. But by the end,&nbsp;
he realizes that isn’t going to cut it anymore.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:03.300
Jake says in the film’s closing narration, "I&nbsp;
can’t save my family by running". This suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:09.180
that he’s accepted Tonowari’s invitation to stay&nbsp;
with the Metkayina, rather than flee again. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.980
death of Neteyam has changed Jake’s perspective,&nbsp;
and he now knows that fighting back is the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:18.388
way to earn peace. However, he doesn’t give&nbsp;
us many real clues about his next move.

00:16:18.388 --> 00:16:19.376
"Let's get it done."

00:16:19.376 --> 00:16:24.180
Staying with the Metkayina could have some&nbsp;
benefits for sure, but so could moving on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:30.660
yet another Na’vi clan. If the RDA is to be fully&nbsp;
defeated, Pandora needs to be united. Staying on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:36.060
the move also isn't a bad idea, and it isn’t the&nbsp;
same as running away. Whatever happens, the Sullys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:40.440
have their work cut out for them, but they seem&nbsp;
more than up to the task after "The Way of Water."

